pairs.perturbed_problem:
For a real number $\alpha$, if the system
$$\begin{bmatrix}
\alpha^{2}-\alpha-1 & -1 & 1\\
-1 & \alpha^{2}-\alpha-1 & -1\\
1 & -1 & \alpha^{2}-\alpha-1
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}x\\y\\z\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}1\\-1\\1\end{bmatrix}$$
of linear equations has infinitely many solutions, then what is the sum of all possible values of $1+\alpha+\alpha^{2}$?
